<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kanesatake</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kanesatake is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> community on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> river west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, next to the town of Oka. It originated in a Sulpician mission founded in the early 18th century, and its people's long dispute over land with the Sulpicians and the town led in 1990 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-crisis">Oka crisis</a>, a 78-day armed stand-off over plans to extend a golf course onto a Mohawk burial ground.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanesatake.jpg" alt="Kanesatake" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gordalmighty · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanesatake_Quebec_location_diagram.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kanesatake, in Mohawk Kanehsatà꞉ke, meaning sandy place, lies on the shore of the Lake of Two Mountains at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> rivers, about 48 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. The mission village was also known by its Algonquin name Oka, meaning pickerel, and the town of Oka grew up around the Mohawk land on ground the Sulpicians sold to settlers. A pine forest and a cemetery long used by the Mohawk lie outside the community's present territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1717 the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> a tract 9 miles long by 9 miles wide, on condition that they leave the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. It was founded as a seigneury of the Sulpician Order for about 300 Christian Mohawk, 100 Algonquin and 250 Nipissing. The Sulpicians later obtained legal title, while the Mohawk believed the land was held in trust for them. In 1787 Chief Aughneeta wrote to Sir John Johnson, the British superintendent general of Indian Affairs, that they had come only after the king promised them a deed.</p><p>Protests continued. In 1868 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> chose the 22-year-old Sosé Onasakenrat as chief; that year he presented their claim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> in vain, and he and many others joined the Methodist Episcopal Church. After his death in 1881 the Sulpicians arranged a move to what is now Wahta Mohawk Territory near Muskoka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, but only 20 percent of the Mohawk went. In 1910 the Canadian Supreme Court upheld the claim of the Sulpician Order.</p><p>When Oka approved a golf-course extension onto the pines and cemetery in 1990, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> barricaded a dirt road. The Sûreté du Québec raided the barricade on 11 July 1990 and a police officer was killed in an exchange of gunfire. The Canadian Army sent 3,700 troops, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a> blockaded the Mercier Bridge in support. The International Federation of Human Rights concluded that neither government had negotiated in good faith. Those arrested at the barricades were acquitted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kanesatake is classified by the federal government as an interim land base rather than a reserve under the Indian Act, and its people govern themselves. Chiefs were once chosen by clan mothers; since 1991 a grand chief and six council chiefs have been elected, while many people keep to the longhouse. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a> its people hold rights to the Doncaster 17 Indian Reserve.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kanesatake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many Catholic converts moved to the mission villages of Kahnawake and <b>Kanesatake</b> near Montreal.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>Mohawk communities today include Akwesasne, Kahnawake and <b>Kanesatake</b>, scene of the 1990 Oka crisis, along with Six Nations and Tyendinaga in Ontario.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
